('se-topic-1','se-realworld','topic',1,'System Architecture & Design Patterns','Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to reverse. This section covers the modular monolith, clean architecture, DDD, and VSA — the defaults that fit a 3-person ERP team.','{"section":1,"subsections":5,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-1.1","title":"The Core Idea: Architecture = Expensive-to-Reverse Decisions","description":"Architecture is the set of decisions expensive to reverse — system boundaries, data models, and dependency direction — not folder structure."},{"id":"se-topic-1.2","title":"Recommended Default: Modular Monolith + Clean Architecture","description":"For a 3-person team, a modular monolith with strict internal modules is the default — simpler to deploy and easier to extract later."},{"id":"se-topic-1.3","title":"Three Architecture Styles to Combine","description":"Combine Clean Architecture (inward dependencies), DDD (ubiquitous language + bounded contexts), and Vertical Slice Architecture (feature-first folders)."},{"id":"se-topic-1.4","title":"Architecture Decision Framework","description":"Start with a modular monolith; extract a service only when a module has a proven, independent reason to deploy or scale."},{"id":"se-topic-1.5","title":"Architecture for Your ERP This Week","description":"Draw a C4 Context diagram, identify bounded contexts, and create one module per context with clearly defined interfaces."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-2','se-realworld','topic',2,'Development (Technical) Documentation','The single source of truth for your team. Covers the documents every real project needs, plus practical SRS, ADR, OpenAPI, and C4 templates.','{"section":2,"subsections":5,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-2.1","title":"Documents Every Real Project Needs","description":"BRD, SRS, ADRs, SDD, OpenAPI, runbooks, README, and test plan — each with a distinct purpose and audience."},{"id":"se-topic-2.2","title":"SRS Template (Practical Version)","description":"A practical outline for specifying what the system must do: functional and non-functional requirements, interfaces, and data."},{"id":"se-topic-2.3","title":"ADR Template","description":"Every expensive-to-reverse decision gets an Architecture Decision Record: status, context, decision, consequences, alternatives."},{"id":"se-topic-2.4","title":"OpenAPI / API Documentation","description":"Write an openapi.yaml as the contract; document every endpoint and keep the spec agreed between frontend and backend."},{"id":"se-topic-2.5","title":"C4 Model Diagrams","description":"Communicate architecture at four zoom levels — context, container, component, code — using Structurizr, Mermaid, or Excalidraw."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-3','se-realworld','topic',3,'Business & Client Documentation','Engineering docs help build it right; business docs help build the right thing. Covers BRD, user manuals, SOW, and SLA templates.','{"section":3,"subsections":4,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-3.1","title":"BRD Template","description":"Executive summary, scope, stakeholders, requirements, success metrics, timeline, risks, and approval."},{"id":"se-topic-3.2","title":"User Manual / Training Guide","description":"Per module: what it does, who uses it, step-by-step workflows, common errors, and support contacts."},{"id":"se-topic-3.3","title":"Client Proposal / Statement of Work (SOW)","description":"Scope, deliverables, timeline, cost, assumptions, change control, acceptance criteria, and support/maintenance."},{"id":"se-topic-3.4","title":"SLA Template (for Client Support)","description":"Severity-based response and resolution targets, from P1 critical (15 min / 4 hr) to P4 low."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-4','se-realworld','topic',4,'Tech Stack & Deployment Decision Framework','How to evaluate your current stack and choose where to host beyond Render. Covers framework, backend, and deployment-route decisions plus an environment strategy.','{"section":4,"subsections":6,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-4.1","title":"Evaluating Your Current Stack","description":"Vanilla JS + Node + Supabase + Render is a reasonable MVP start; know each layer’s role before changing it."},{"id":"se-topic-4.2","title":"When to Consider Changing Frontend Frameworks","description":"Move to React or Vue when reusable components, state, and routing grow; migrate one isolated module first."},{"id":"se-topic-4.3","title":"When to Consider Changing Backend Approach","description":"Add TypeScript first; consider typed languages only when strict transactional consistency or heavy CPU demands it."},{"id":"se-topic-4.4","title":"Deployment Route Options","description":"PaaS, CaaS, IaaS, managed Kubernetes, serverless, and VPS — understand the menu and tradeoffs before choosing."},{"id":"se-topic-4.5","title":"Decision Framework for Hosting","description":"Ask operational time, statefulness, region, compliance, traffic, HA, and budget in order; stay on Render until you hit real limits."},{"id":"se-topic-4.6","title":"Environment Strategy","description":"Maintain development, staging, and production environments; never host production and staging on the same server or database."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-5','se-realworld','topic',5,'Post-Deployment Support & SRE','Deployment is where the real responsibility starts. Covers observability, SLOs and error budgets, incident response, runbooks, toil reduction, and a production-readiness checklist.','{"section":5,"subsections":6,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-5.1","title":"The Four Pillars of Observability","description":"Metrics, logs, traces, and alerts — each tells you something different about system health."},{"id":"se-topic-5.2","title":"Define SLOs, SLIs, and Error Budgets","description":"SLIs measure, SLOs set targets, SLAs promise contractually, and error budgets cap how much unreliability you can spend."},{"id":"se-topic-5.3","title":"Incident Response for Small Teams","description":"Detect, triage, mitigate, resolve, postmortem — a blameless five-step loop with affordable tools."},{"id":"se-topic-5.4","title":"Runbook Template","description":"Symptoms, immediate checks, mitigation, resolution, and escalation — documented so anyone can respond."},{"id":"se-topic-5.5","title":"Reducing Toil","description":"Keep toil under 30% of operational time; automate backups, certs, dependency updates, and rollbacks."},{"id":"se-topic-5.6","title":"Production Readiness Checklist","description":"Before a major release: monitoring, logs, backups, rollback, secrets, security scan, load test, runbooks, and client docs."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-6','se-realworld','topic',6,'Engineering Discipline: Quality, Security, Maintainability','The habits that keep a growing system reliable: version control, code review, the testing pyramid, security (OWASP API Top 10), database design, technical debt, and CI/CD gates.','{"section":6,"subsections":8,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-6.1","title":"Version Control and Branching","description":"Use GitHub Flow or trunk-based development; GitFlow is usually overkill for a 3-person team."},{"id":"se-topic-6.2","title":"Code Review","description":"Small PRs (under 400 lines), 4–24h turnaround, and a PR template: what changed, why, how to test."},{"id":"se-topic-6.3","title":"Testing Strategy: The Pyramid","description":"~70% unit, ~20% integration, ~10% E2E — buy the right confidence at the right cost."},{"id":"se-topic-6.4","title":"Contract Testing","description":"Use Pact or validate against your OpenAPI spec on every PR so frontend and backend agree on the API shape."},{"id":"se-topic-6.5","title":"Security: OWASP Top 10 for APIs (2025/2026)","description":"Priorities: BOLA/IDOR, broken auth, property-level auth, resource consumption, function-level auth, SSRF, misconfig, and supply chain."},{"id":"se-topic-6.6","title":"Database Design and Indexing","description":"B-tree indexes on keys and filtered columns, composite indexes (equality then range), and expand-and-contract schema evolution."},{"id":"se-topic-6.7","title":"Technical Debt Management","description":"Track debt in an inventory, rate by impact/effort, and allocate 15–20% of sprint capacity to paying it down."},{"id":"se-topic-6.8","title":"CI/CD and Quality Gates","description":"Lint → scan → unit → integration → build → staging → smoke → approve → deploy, with rollback ready."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-7','se-realworld','topic',7,'Hands-On Projects Applied to Your ERP','Eight projects that turn theory into applied skill on your ERP — from drawing architecture to evaluating new hosting.','{"section":7,"subsections":8,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-7.1","title":"Project 1: Draw Your Architecture","description":"Create C4 Context and Container diagrams; identify bounded contexts; write ADR-001 on the modular monolith."},{"id":"se-topic-7.2","title":"Project 2: Documentation Sprint","description":"Write an SRS for the next module, a BRD for one client, an OpenAPI spec, and publish with Swagger UI or Scalar."},{"id":"se-topic-7.3","title":"Project 3: Add Observability","description":"Structured logging; connect to Better Stack/Logtail free tier; alerts for 5xx + latency; two runbooks."},{"id":"se-topic-7.4","title":"Project 4: Testing Ramp-Up","description":"Jest unit tests, Supertest integration for auth + one business API, one Playwright E2E, and GitHub Actions on every PR."},{"id":"se-topic-7.5","title":"Project 5: Security Hardening","description":"Audit against OWASP API Top 10; per-resource ownership checks; rate limiting; npm audit and triage."},{"id":"se-topic-7.6","title":"Project 6: Database Optimization","description":"Find the 5 slowest queries; add indexes and measure; document the indexing strategy."},{"id":"se-topic-7.7","title":"Project 7: Deployment Portability","description":"Dockerize the Node.js API; run with Docker Compose + Postgres; document one-command local full-stack run."},{"id":"se-topic-7.8","title":"Project 8: Evaluate New Hosting","description":"Deploy a staging copy to Railway or Fly.io; compare cost, cold-start, and deploy experience vs Render; write an ADR."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-8','se-realworld','topic',8,'Weekly Study Plan','A 12-week plan (19–26 hrs/week) covering architecture and docs (Month 1), quality/security/operations (Month 2), and deployment/scalability/client engineering (Month 3).','{"section":8,"subsections":4,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-8.1","title":"Month 1: Architecture, Documentation, and Database","description":"C4/DDD basics, Clean Architecture/VSA, SRS/BRD/ADR writing, and database design/indexing — ~22 hrs/week."},{"id":"se-topic-8.2","title":"Month 2: Quality, Security, and Operations","description":"Testing pyramid, CI/CD and code review, OWASP API Top 10, and observability/SRE — ~22 hrs/week."},{"id":"se-topic-8.3","title":"Month 3: Deployment, Scalability, and Client Engineering","description":"Deployment routes, caching/performance, client docs/SLAs, and technical debt — ~22 hrs/week."},{"id":"se-topic-8.4","title":"Daily Rhythm Suggestion","description":"Deep reading + hands-on blocks on no-class days; lighter review on class days; plan the next week on Sundays."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-9','se-realworld','topic',9,'Recommended Resources','Curated books, video courses, official docs, articles, and tools from the guide — each tagged to the topics it covers.','{"section":9,"subsections":4,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-9.1","title":"Books","description":"Ten foundational books on architecture, design, data, DDD, production resilience, and trade-off analysis."},{"id":"se-topic-9.2","title":"Video Courses / Channels","description":"ByteByteGo, ArjanCodes, Fireship, freeCodeCamp, and Udemy courses on architecture and system design."},{"id":"se-topic-9.3","title":"Official Documentation and Guides","description":"C4, OpenAPI, OWASP, Google SRE, Docker, GitHub Actions, Jest, Playwright, Supabase, Render, Railway, Fly.io."},{"id":"se-topic-9.4","title":"Articles, Tutorials, and Tools","description":"Rate limiting, testing taxonomies, SRS templates, branching strategies, and a tools table for the ERP."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-10','se-realworld','topic',10,'Quick Reference: Decisions You Will Face Soon','Short answers to the recurring decisions: framework, microservices, hosting, TypeScript, testing volume, and technical debt.','{"section":10,"subsections":6,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-10.1","title":"Should we move from vanilla JS to a framework?","description":"Yes if the UI is growing or state is hard to manage; start with Vue or React in one isolated module."},{"id":"se-topic-10.2","title":"Should we split the monolith into microservices?","description":"Not yet; extract a service only for independent deployment, scaling, or team ownership."},{"id":"se-topic-10.3","title":"Should we move off Render?","description":"Evaluate Railway/Fly.io when Render limits hurt; move to a cloud only for VPCs, networking, or managed services."},{"id":"se-topic-10.4","title":"Should we add TypeScript?","description":"Yes, as soon as possible — the highest-ROI change for a growing Node.js project."},{"id":"se-topic-10.5","title":"How much testing is enough?","description":"Start with unit tests for core logic and integration tests for APIs; aim for meaningful coverage, not a number."},{"id":"se-topic-10.6","title":"How do we handle technical debt?","description":"Track it, prioritize by impact/effort, and spend 15–20% of every sprint on it."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-11','se-realworld','topic',11,'Conclusion: Your Path Forward','You are already running a software business. Pick one project per week, tie every concept to your ERP, write down decisions, and review them monthly.','{"section":11,"subsections":0}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-12','se-realworld','topic',12,'Deep Dive: Software Design Patterns','Design patterns are a shared vocabulary, not rules. Covers creational, structural, behavioral, and architectural patterns plus anti-patterns and sources.','{"section":12,"subsections":7,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-12.1","title":"Why Patterns Matter for Your ERP","description":"Patterns reduce reinventing broken wheels: object creation, single instances, adding behavior, and adapting interfaces."},{"id":"se-topic-12.2","title":"Creational Patterns","description":"Singleton, Factory, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype — dealing with object creation."},{"id":"se-topic-12.3","title":"Structural Patterns","description":"Adapter, Bridge, Composite, Decorator, Facade, Flyweight, Proxy — how objects and classes compose."},{"id":"se-topic-12.4","title":"Behavioral Patterns","description":"Chain of Responsibility, Command, Iterator, Mediator, Observer, State, Strategy, Template Method, Visitor — communication between objects."},{"id":"se-topic-12.5","title":"Architectural Patterns (Beyond GoF)","description":"MVC/MVVM, Repository, Unit of Work, CQRS, Event Sourcing, Saga, Outbox — system-level patterns."},{"id":"se-topic-12.6","title":"Anti-Patterns to Avoid","description":"God Object, Spaghetti Code, Golden Hammer, Premature Abstraction, Magic Numbers — and their better alternatives."},{"id":"se-topic-12.7","title":"Recommended Sources for Design Patterns","description":"Refactoring Guru, Mosh Hamedani, SourceMaking, and Head First Design Patterns."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-13','se-realworld','topic',13,'Deep Dive: Types of Software Testing','Testing is buying the right confidence at the right cost. Covers the pyramid, unit, integration, E2E, contract, performance, security, and a testing strategy for the ERP.','{"section":13,"subsections":10,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-13.1","title":"The Testing Pyramid Expanded","description":"~70/20/10 unit/integration/E2E with cost and confidence per layer; the Testing Trophy favors static analysis + integration."},{"id":"se-topic-13.2","title":"Unit Testing","description":"Test a single function/class in isolation — best for pure business logic, edge cases, and refactoring safety nets."},{"id":"se-topic-13.3","title":"Integration Testing","description":"Test modules together with real DB/API calls — best for endpoint behavior and repository/service interaction."},{"id":"se-topic-13.4","title":"End-to-End (E2E) Testing","description":"Drive the browser through complete workflows — best for critical user journeys; keep minimal and high-value."},{"id":"se-topic-13.5","title":"Contract Testing","description":"Verify frontend and backend agree on the API contract — use Pact or validate against OpenAPI on every PR."},{"id":"se-topic-13.6","title":"Performance and Load Testing","description":"Load, stress, spike, soak, endurance testing with k6, Artillery, JMeter — run before client go-lives and month-end peaks."},{"id":"se-topic-13.7","title":"Security Testing","description":"SAST, DAST, SCA, penetration testing, and secrets scanning — layered tools for finding vulnerabilities."},{"id":"se-topic-13.8","title":"Other Important Test Types","description":"Regression, smoke, usability, accessibility, mutation, and snapshot testing — each for a specific moment."},{"id":"se-topic-13.9","title":"Test Strategy for Your ERP","description":"Static analysis first, then unit for business logic, integration for API+DB, E2E for critical flows, security scans in CI, and load tests before launches."},{"id":"se-topic-13.10","title":"Recommended Sources for Testing","description":"Atlassian, Tricentis, Microsoft, CircleCI, Jest, Playwright, k6, OWASP ZAP, and Pact docs."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-14','se-realworld','topic',14,'Deep Dive: Core Production Concepts','The small but essential concepts that separate a demo from a production system: rate limiting, caching, idempotency, pagination, background jobs, circuit breakers, and feature flags.','{"section":14,"subsections":7,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-14.1","title":"Rate Limiting","description":"Control request volume per time window with fixed window, sliding window, token bucket, or leaky bucket algorithms."},{"id":"se-topic-14.2","title":"Caching Strategies","description":"Cache-Aside, Write-Through, Write-Behind, Refresh-Ahead — choose by staleness tolerance and freshness needs."},{"id":"se-topic-14.3","title":"Idempotency","description":"An Idempotency-Key header makes retries and double-clicks safe — essential for invoices, charges, and payroll runs."},{"id":"se-topic-14.4","title":"Pagination","description":"Never return unbounded lists — use cursor pagination for large sorted datasets, offset for small admin lists."},{"id":"se-topic-14.5","title":"Background Jobs","description":"Move long or risky operations (reports, bulk email, imports, slow API calls) to queues with BullMQ, Agenda, or Supabase Edge Functions."},{"id":"se-topic-14.6","title":"Circuit Breakers","description":"Stop calling a failing external service so the failure doesn’t cascade — closed, open, half-open states."},{"id":"se-topic-14.7","title":"Feature Flags","description":"Deploy without exposing to users — gradual rollouts and quick rollbacks via LaunchDarkly, Flagsmith, or simple env vars."}]}'::jsonb),
('se-topic-15','se-realworld','topic',15,'Sources and References','The synthesized source categories behind this guide: architecture, design patterns, testing, security, databases, SRE, deployment, rate limiting, documentation, and code review.','{"section":15,"subsections":7,"outline":[{"id":"se-topic-15.1","title":"Architecture and Design","description":"Richards & Ford, Clean Architecture, Domain-Driven Design, Jedrzejewski, VickyBytes, Wondel.ai."},{"id":"se-topic-15.2","title":"Design Patterns","description":"Gamma et al. (GoF), Refactoring Guru, Mosh Hamedani, and the “8 Design Patterns” video."},{"id":"se-topic-15.3","title":"Testing","description":"Cohn (testing pyramid), Atlassian, Tricentis, Microsoft, CircleCI, and Pact."},{"id":"se-topic-15.4","title":"Security","description":"OWASP API Security Top 10, Zuplo, WorkOS, and Parasoft."},{"id":"se-topic-15.5","title":"Databases","description":"Kleppmann, Nagurkar, JusDB, and Codelit indexing guides."},{"id":"se-topic-15.6","title":"SRE and Operations","description":"Google SRE Book, Release It!, Better Stack, incident.io, and The Phoenix Project."},{"id":"se-topic-15.7","title":"Deployment and Infrastructure","description":"Render, Railway, Fly.io, Docker, and Akuity GitOps guides."}]}'::jsonb),
